40.0,\\\\n      \\"payback_years\\": null\\\\n    }\\\\n  ],\\\\n  \\"kpi_sets\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\"name\\": \\"Cumulative Revenue (HKD M)\\",\\\\n      \\"unit\\": \\"HKD M\\",\\\\n      \\"quarterly_targets_8_quarters\\": [8.0, 8.498, 9.027, 9.589, 10.185, 10.819, 11.493, 12.208]\\\\n    }\\\\n  ],\\\\n  \\"audit_trail\\": {\\\\n    \\"intermediate_calculation_used_to_derive_annual_net_cashflows\\": \\"Gross_profit (Revenue*gross_margin%) minus Occupancy (Revenue*15%) minus Staff (1.2 constant). Other OPEX assumed 0 because not provided.\\",\\\\n    \\"no_invented_numbers_rule\\": \\"All numeric inputs above are taken directly from the provided assumptions; no additional operating costs or margins were added.\\"\\\\n  }\\\\n}\\\\n```', tool_call_id='call_pf8ByV6mH1YHmilQ3DXa3EMR')]})"}, "name": "task", "event": "on_tool_end"}, {"data": {"input": {"description": "Research market size, growth, competitors, and regulatory context for the project:\\n{\\n  \\"project_name\\": \\"Causeway Bay Flagship Store Expansion\\",\\n  \\"project_type\\": \\"CAPEX\\",\\n  \\"sponsor\\": \\"Alice Wong, Chief Commercial Officer\\",\\n  \\"project_manager\\": \\"Ben Lam, Senior Project Manager\\",\\n  \\"business_unit\\": \\"Retail Operations\\",\\n  \\"capex_hkd_millions\\": 5.0,\\n  \\"description\\": \\"Expand the existing Causeway Bay store from 3,000 sq ft to 6,000 sq ft by taking over the adjacent unit. The expanded space will house a new premium product line and in-store experience zone to drive higher average basket size and foot traffic.\\",\\n  \\"assumptions\\": {\\n    \\"lease_term_years\\": 5,\\n    \\"fit_out_cost_hkd_millions\\": 3.0,\\n    \\"equipment_cost_hkd_millions\\": 1.5,\\n    \\"contingency_hkd_millions\\": 0.5,\\n    \\"revenue_year1_hkd_millions\\": 8.0,\\n    \\"revenue_growth_pct_pa\\": 10,\\n    \\"gross_margin_pct\\": 42,\\n    \\"occupancy_cost_pct_of_revenue\\": 15,\\n    \\"staff_cost_hkd_millions_pa\\": 1.2,\\n    \\"opening_date\\": \\"Q3 2026\\"\\n  },\\n  \\"attachments\\": [],\\n  \\"known_market_data\\": {\\n    \\"hong_kong_retail_sales_growth_2025_pct\\": 4.5,\\n    \\"luxury_retail_segment_growth_pct\\": 6.2,\\n    \\"causeway_bay_foot_traffic_index\\": 98\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn in markdown: (1) HK overall retail outlook (size/growth, citations), (2) luxury/premium segment trend, (3) Causeway Bay retail/foot-traffic context (any indices/rents), (4) key competitors/nearby brands and their strategies (expansion/omnichannel/store experience), (5) regulatory/legal considerations for retail fit-out in HK (lease, retail licensing, fire safety, signage/advertising, consumer protection), (6) risks/trends specifically relevant to a flagship expansion.", "subagent_type": "research-agent"}, "output": "Command(update={'files': {}, 'messages': [ToolMessage(content=\\"## (1) HK overall retail outlook (size/growth, citations)\\\\n**Overall demand / growth**\\\\n- **2025 total retail sales value grew ~+1% YoY (after a ~-7.3% in 2024)**; 2025 retail sales value was **provisionally estimated at ~HK$380.5bn**.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong's retail sales edge up 1% in 2025 after 7.3% decline 2024”* — SCMP (from search result)  \\\\n- **Near-term momentum into 2026 is improving**: Deloitte forecast **retail sales to rise up to ~+8% in 2026 to around HK$410bn** (based on strengthening consumer confidence and rising inbound visitors).  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail outlook 2025 2026 tourism recovery foot traffic retail sales growth Oxford Economics Deloitte KPMG report”* — Deloitte press-room result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**Monthly signal (context only)**\\\\n- TradingEconomics shows **retail sales grew +9.8% YoY in March 2026** (with month-to-month volatility).  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail market size 2024 2025 total retail sales value growth rate 2025 2026 forecast”* — TradingEconomics result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**Market intelligence takeaway for a CAPEX expansion**\\\\n- The market is **recovering but uneven**: headline growth can be positive, but store-level performance depends heavily on **tourism/inbound mix, lease terms, and district footfall**.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## (2) Luxury / premium segment trend\\\\n- A useful proxy for premium demand: Hong Kong luxury sales have shown **upward trends into 2026** (category reporting in luxury-focused outlets). Example:\\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong luxury retail market growth 2024 2025 2026 segment trend premium goods spending Mainland visitors”* — Rapaport result: **luxury sales across retail categories rose ~13% to HK$33.93bn in March** (as reported in the search result).\\\\n- **Mainstream “premium/experience” retail trend:** brands increasingly compete on **store experience + omnichannel**, with physical flagship/store upgrades used to stimulate higher basket size and conversion.\\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail competitors Causeway Bay flagship store expansion premium brands omnichannel store experience”* — KPMG/KPMG-linked omnichannel realignment study (from search result)\\\\n\\\\n**Flag for finance/writer agents**\\\\n- Luxury/premium growth exists, but **visibility depends on inbound flows (tourists/Mainland visitors), brand allocation, and discretionary spending confidence**.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## (3) Causeway Bay retail / foot-traffic context (indices/rents)\\\\n**Prime-district vacancy (benchmark indicator)**\\\\n- Cushman & Wakefield “Retail MarketBeat Q3 2025” (search result) reported a **district vacancy rate benchmark**:\\\\n  - **Causeway Bay vacancy ~7.9% in Q3 2025** (vs **9.7% Q2** in the same report’s summary for the overall street vacancy trend).  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Causeway Bay retail market rents vacancy rates 2024 2025 prime retail streets SFC survey Hysan Sogo Times Square footfall”* — Cushman & Wakefield PDF result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**Footfall proxy (direct index data)**\\\\n- For Causeway Bay specifically, the search results did **not** return a clean, authoritative “Causeway Bay foot traffic index” comparable to your input **(98)**. Instead, results referenced **coverage about footfall variability** at major nodes (e.g., Times Square) suggesting **competing retail clusters nearby may divert shoppers**.\\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Causeway Bay foot traffic index retail footfall study 2024 2025 Times Square Hysan Sogo”* — Dimsumdaily result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**How to use this in the business case**\\\\n- You can support the expansion thesis by combining:\\\\n  1) **district vacancy/renewal dynamics** (benchmark), and  \\\\n  2) **experience/tenant mix strategy** (to convert footfall effectively, even if footfall shifts between complexes).\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## (4) Key competitors / nearby brands & strategies (expansion/omnichannel/experience)\\\\nFrom the search results, notable nearby/portfolio competitor signals include:\\\\n\\\\n- **CHANEL (Causeway Bay)**: reported to have **opened/leased a new prime Causeway Bay store** (positioning aligned with inbound/tourist luxury demand).  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail competitors Causeway Bay flagship store expansion premium brands omnichannel store experience”* — SCMP result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n- **7-Eleven Hong Kong (Causeway Bay concept store)**: example of brands using **concept-store formats** and **customer-experience redesign** rather than only traditional shelving-based convenience retail.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same as above — DFI Retail Group result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**Omnichannel / experience trend**\\\\n- KPMG-linked research emphasizes **omnichannel realignment** (store as conversion/experience node, not just a sales point), consistent with your “in-store experience zone” approach.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same as above — KPMG omnichannel study result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n> Note: Your business case is “premium product line + experience zone”; the competitor evidence above supports the **direction of travel** even if we didn’t extract a full list of every nearby flagship in the search outputs.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## (5) Regulatory/legal considerations for retail fit-out in HK\\\\nBelow are **regulatory areas flagged in the search results** (high-level, for writer/finance to confirm with counsel/landlord/FS team):\\\\n\\\\n### A) Lease / landlord-tenant & assignment / repair & fit-out approvals\\\\n- **Landlord and Tenant (Consolidation) Ordinance (Cap. 7)** (framework for tenancies and tenant protections).  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong shop lease regulation Landlord and Tenant (Consolidation) Ordinance retail leases assignment repair works fit out approvals”* — Cap.7 result (from search)\\\\n- Practical implication: fit-out and reinstatement obligations, approvals, and dispute risk should be verified under the **specific lease** and any **landlord consent conditions**.\\\\n\\\\n### B) Fire safety for commercial premises (major fit-out trigger)\\\\n- **Fire Safety (Commercial Premises) Ordinance (Cap. 502)**: the core statutory basis for fire safety controls in prescribed commercial premises/buildings.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail store fit-out regulatory requirements fire safety building ordinances fire services department retail premises signage advertising regulations Consumer Goods Safety Ordinance”* — Cap.502 result (from search)\\\\n- Government/building management guidance sites also emphasize **fire safety design and compliance** processes.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same — Building Dept / HK FSD pages (from search)\\\\n\\\\n### C) Building department safety / inspections\\\\n- Hong Kong Building Department provides **fire safety** and safety-inspection guidance relevant to building works and compliance checks.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same as above — BD “fire safety” result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n### D) Signage / advertising & consumer-facing compliance\\\\n- Search surfaced **signage/advertising regulatory consideration** as a relevant category, but the returned snippet list did **not** include a specific “signage act/regulation” citation in the results you see here.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same — (signage/advertising appears in the query-driven result set, but confirm exact legal basis in a follow-up targeted search if needed.)\\\\n\\\\n### E) Retail licensing / need to operate\\\\n- A Hong Kong government Q&A (search result) indicates that **there are generally no specific licensing requirements for operating a retail shop**, but this must be cross-checked against your **product categories** (e.g., regulated goods) and any **shop-permit requirements** tied to the tenancy.  \\\\n  - Citation (query used): same — Info.gov.hk result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n**Regulatory risk flag (for CAPEX)**\\\\n- In retail fit-outs, **fire safety approval/design** and **building works compliance** typically represent the highest schedule/regulatory execution risk.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## (6) Risks / trends specifically relevant to a flagship expansion (HK)\\\\n1. **Footfall reallocation risk (district-level competition)**\\\\n   - Causeway Bay shopping often competes within adjacent clusters (e.g., large malls/anchors nearby). The search results suggest **footfall can shift between complexes**—a flagship upgrade may not automatically offset macro foot-traffic fluctuations.  \\\\n   - Citation (query used): *“Causeway Bay foot traffic index retail footfall study 2024 2025 Times Square Hysan Sogo”* — Dimsumdaily result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n2. **Vacancy and leasing environment volatility**\\\\n   - Vacancy rates can move quarter-to-quarter; if vacancy rises or landlord release terms worsen, the ability to secure/retain premium space and tenant mix can be impacted.  \\\\n   - Citation (query used): *“Causeway Bay retail market rents vacancy rates 2024 2025 prime retail streets SFC survey Hysan...”* — Cushman & Wakefield result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n3. **Regulatory/approval schedule risk**\\\\n   - Fit-out works can be slowed by **fire safety (Cap. 502) compliance** and required approvals/testing for commercial premises.  \\\\n   - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail store fit-out regulatory requirements fire safety...”* — Cap.502 + HK FSD pages (from search)\\\\n\\\\n4. **Gross-margin pressure from premium positioning**\\\\n   - Premium product lines may lift average basket size, but also can increase **inventory risk, promotional intensity, and shrink/handling costs**—affecting margin outcomes. (This is a derived risk; market citations support the premium/exo experience trend rather than margin mechanics.)\\\\n\\\\n5. **Omnichannel execution risk**\\\\n   - Flagships increasingly must integrate with omnichannel (e.g., click & collect, loyalty, inventory visibility). Failure to execute can weaken ROI even when footfall improves.  \\\\n   - Citation (query used): *“Hong Kong retail competitors Causeway Bay flagship store expansion premium brands omnichannel store experience”* — KPMG omnichannel study result (from search)\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n### Search queries used (for traceability)\\\\n1. **“Hong Kong retail market size 2024 2025 total retail sales value growth rate 2025 2026 forecast”**  \\\\n2. **“Hong Kong retail outlook 2025 2026 tourism recovery foot traffic retail sales growth Oxford Economics Deloitte KPMG report”**  \\\\n3. **“Hong Kong luxury retail market growth 2024 2025 2026 segment trend premium goods spending Mainland visitors”**  \\\\n4. **“Causeway Bay retail market rents vacancy rates 2024 2025 prime retail streets SFC survey Hysan Sogo Times Square footfall”**  \\\\n5. **“Causeway Bay foot traffic index retail footfall study 2024 2025 Times Square Hysan Sogo”**  \\\\n6. **“Hong Kong retail competitors Causeway Bay flagship store expansion premium brands omnichannel store experience”**  \\\\n7. **“Hong Kong retail store fit-out regulatory requirements fire safety building ordinances fire services department retail premises signage advertising regulations Consumer Goods Safety Ordinance”**  \\\\n8. **“Hong Kong shop lease regulation Landlord and Tenant (Consolidation) Ordinance retail leases assignment repair works fit out approvals”**\\\\n\\\\nIf you want, I can run 1–2 **follow-up, more targeted** searches to (a) find a stronger **Causeway Bay footfall/retail rent index source**, and (b) pin down **specific signage/advertising legal requirements** with exact citations.\\", tool_call_id='call_FZuJqBsEpt6z7w1NzQUdZFvd')]})"}, "name": "task", "event": "on_tool_end"}]	2026-05-14 14:57:39.585532	2026-05-14 14:58:28.938721
